{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>For I have known him.</b> A term of affection — as in ״מוֹדַעַת לְאִישָׁהּ״ (Ruth 2), ״הֲלֹא בֹעַז מֹדַעְתָּנוּ״ (Ruth 3), ״וָאֵדָעֲךָ בְּשֵׁם״ (Exodus 33). And indeed the root sense of all of them is nothing but the language of knowing — for one who loves a person draws him near and knows him and is familiar with him. And why 'I have known him'?" "<b>So that he may charge.</b> Because he charges his sons concerning Me to keep My ways. And if you explain it as the Targum does — 'I know of him that he will charge his sons, etc.' — לְמַעַן does not fit the language." "<b>May charge.</b> Present-tense, as in ״כָּכָה יַעֲשֶׂה אִיּוֹב״ (Job 1)." "<b>To bring.</b> Thus he charges his sons: Keep the way of HaShem, so that HaShem may bring upon Abraham, etc. — it is not said 'upon the house of Abraham' but 'upon Abraham.' This teaches: whoever establishes a righteous son — it is as though he did not die."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:19.552039349Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}